(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["chem-11-06"] = {"chem-11-06-m01":{"idea":"Strong bonds hold atoms inside a molecule. Weaker forces act between separate molecules.","steps":["The forces between molecules are called intermolecular forces.","They are weak, about 1 to 40 kJ/mol.","Covalent bonds inside a molecule are much stronger, about 200 to 500 kJ/mol.","Intermolecular forces decide the boiling point and the state of matter."],"others":"Gravitational forces between molecules are negligible. Nuclear forces act inside the nucleus. Intramolecular forces are stronger, but they act inside the molecule, not between molecules.","answer":"intermolecular forces, which are much weaker than the forces inside the molecules"},"chem-11-06-m02":{"idea":"Only one kind of intermolecular force exists in every substance, even in non-polar ones.","steps":["The electron cloud of a molecule keeps moving.","At one instant it gives a temporary dipole.","This dipole induces a dipole in the neighbour, and they attract.","This is the dispersion (London) force, and every molecule has it."],"others":"Hydrogen bonds need H bonded to N, O or F. Ion–dipole forces need an ion. Dipole–dipole forces need permanent polar molecules. Non-polar molecules have none of these.","answer":"dispersion (London) forces"},"chem-11-06-m03":{"idea":"Compare boiling points by looking at the forces between molecules.","steps":["In water, H is bonded to small, very electronegative O.","This makes strong O–H···O hydrogen bonds between molecules.","H₂S has only weak dipole–dipole and dispersion forces.","More energy is needed to separate water molecules, so water boils higher."],"others":"H₂S does not form strong hydrogen bonds. Sulfur is less electronegative than oxygen, not more. Water has a smaller molar mass, but that would lower its boiling point, so it cannot be the reason.","answer":"molecules of water are held together by hydrogen bonds"},"chem-11-06-m04":{"idea":"Gas molecules are far apart and move freely.","steps":["In a gas there is a lot of empty space between molecules.","So a gas can be compressed easily.","A gas has no fixed shape or volume, and it mixes with other gases.","Its density is low, far from that of solids."],"others":"Gases always mix. Their density is low, not like solids. A fixed volume with the shape of the container describes a liquid, but a gas has no fixed volume and fills the container.","answer":"they can be compressed easily, as the molecules are far apart"},"chem-11-06-m05":{"idea":"Boyle's law links pressure and volume at a fixed temperature.","steps":["For a fixed amount of gas at constant T, PV = constant.","If P doubles, V halves.","So volume is inversely proportional to pressure."],"others":"Independent of pressure is wrong, since squeezing a gas reduces its volume. Directly proportional to pressure would make V rise as P rises. Directly proportional to the square of pressure is also wrong.","trap":"A graph of V against 1/P is a straight line.","answer":"inversely proportional to the pressure"},"chem-11-06-m06":{"idea":"Charles' law links volume and temperature at a fixed pressure.","steps":["At constant P, V ÷ T = constant.","T must be in kelvin.","So V is directly proportional to the kelvin temperature."],"others":"Volume is not proportional to the Celsius temperature, because 0 °C is not zero volume. It is not independent of temperature. It is not inversely proportional, since gases expand on heating.","trap":"Always change °C to K by adding 273.","answer":"directly proportional to the temperature in kelvin"},"chem-11-06-m07":{"idea":"In Charles' law you must use kelvin, not °C.","steps":["T₁ = 27 + 273 = 300 K. T₂ = 327 + 273 = 600 K.","V₂ ÷ V₁ = T₂ ÷ T₁ = 600 ÷ 300 = 2.","So the volume becomes twice the original."],"others":"The same volume or half would need no change or a fall in temperature. Eleven times comes from a wrong ratio, 327 ÷ 27 = 12.1, which uses °C.","trap":"Using °C gives a wrong ratio.","answer":"twice the original"},"chem-11-06-m08":{"idea":"Avogadro's law connects volume to the number of particles.","steps":["At the same T and P, V is proportional to n (moles).","Equal volumes therefore hold equal numbers of molecules.","The gases can be different, such as H₂ and O₂."],"others":"Equal volumes do not have the same number of atoms, because molecules differ in atoms (He has 1 and O₃ has 3). The mass and density differ for different gases.","answer":"the same number of molecules"},"chem-11-06-m09":{"idea":"The ideal gas equation joins Boyle, Charles and Avogadro laws.","steps":["Boyle: V ∝ 1/P. Charles: V ∝ T. Avogadro: V ∝ n.","Combining gives V ∝ nT/P.","Adding the constant R gives PV = nRT."],"others":"The other forms have the variables in wrong places, so their units do not work. For example P = nRTV would give pressure rising with volume, which is wrong.","answer":"PV = nRT"},"chem-11-06-m10":{"idea":"The value of R depends on the units used for P and V.","steps":["R = 8.314 J/(mol K) when P is in pascal and V in cubic metre.","With P in atmosphere and V in litre, R = 0.08206 L atm/(mol K).","Since 1 atm × 1 L = 101.3 J, the two values agree."],"others":"Pascal and cubic metre give 8.314. Bar and cubic metre give 8.314 × 10⁻⁵. Torr and litre give 62.36.","answer":"atmosphere and litre"},"chem-11-06-m11":{"idea":"Rearrange PV = nRT using n = m/M and density d = m/V.","steps":["n = m/M, so PV = (m/M)RT.","Move terms: M = (m/V)RT/P.","Since m/V = d, M = dRT/P.","Check units: g/L × L atm/(mol K) × K ÷ atm = g/mol."],"others":"The other forms put d, R, T or P in the wrong place. Check units: only dRT/P gives g/mol.","answer":"M = dRT/P"},"chem-11-06-m12":{"idea":"Kinetic theory describes an ideal gas with a few simple assumptions.","steps":["Gas molecules move constantly in random directions.","Collisions with each other and with the walls are elastic, so no energy is lost.","The molecules are tiny, and they do not attract each other.","The pressure comes from collisions with the walls."],"others":"Molecules do not move at one speed or avoid collisions. Their volume is negligible, not large. They do not rest, and they do not attract strongly.","answer":"are in constant random motion, and collide elastically"},"chem-11-06-m13":{"idea":"Temperature measures the average kinetic energy of molecules.","steps":["Average kinetic energy per molecule = (3/2)kT.","It depends only on the absolute temperature.","So at one temperature all gases have the same average kinetic energy.","Light molecules move faster, but heavy ones have the same energy."],"others":"Kinetic energy does not depend on molar mass. It does not depend on volume or pressure of the gas in this way. It depends on temperature only.","answer":"the absolute temperature, and it is the same for all gases at the same temperature"},"chem-11-06-m14":{"idea":"At the same temperature, the average kinetic energy is equal for all gases, so lighter molecules move faster.","steps":["Kinetic energy ½mv² is the same for H₂ and O₂.","So speed v is proportional to 1/√M.","Ratio = √(M of O₂ ÷ M of H₂) = √(32 ÷ 2) = √16 = 4.","Hydrogen molecules are 4 times as fast."],"others":"The speeds are not the same, because the masses differ. 2 times comes from 32 ÷ 16 without a square root. 16 comes from forgetting to take the square root of the 16.","trap":"Use the square root of the mass ratio.","answer":"4 times as large"},"chem-11-06-m15":{"idea":"Ideal behaviour needs molecules that are far apart and fast.","steps":["At low pressure, molecules are far apart.","Their own volume and mutual attraction become negligible.","At high temperature, kinetic energy beats the attractions.","So a real gas is most ideal at high T and low P."],"others":"High pressure squeezes molecules together, so their volume matters. Low temperature lets attractions matter. So the other combinations all include one of these conditions.","answer":"high temperature and low pressure"},"chem-11-06-m16":{"idea":"The compressibility factor Z measures how far a real gas is from ideal.","steps":["For one mole, Z = PV/RT. For n moles, Z = PV/(nRT).","For an ideal gas, PV = nRT, so Z = 1.","Z < 1 means attractions dominate. Z > 1 means molecular volume dominates."],"others":"A Z of 0 for an ideal gas would be wrong, since the ideal value is 1. PT/(nRV) is not a ratio of PV to nRT. nRT/(PV) is the inverse of Z.","answer":"Z = PV/(nRT), which is 1 for an ideal gas"},"chem-11-06-m17":{"idea":"At very high pressure, molecules are squeezed close together.","steps":["The volume of the molecules themselves can no longer be ignored.","Repulsion makes the gas harder to compress than an ideal gas.","The real volume is larger than the ideal volume.","So Z = PV/nRT is greater than 1."],"others":"Z cannot be zero since the volume never becomes zero. Z = 1 is the ideal case. Z < 1 occurs at moderate pressure, where attractions dominate.","answer":"greater than 1, because the volume of the molecules cannot be ignored"},"chem-11-06-m18":{"idea":"Every gas has a critical temperature. Above it, no pressure can make a liquid.","steps":["Above the critical temperature, the kinetic energy is too large for attractions to hold molecules together.","So pressure alone cannot liquefy the gas.","Below that temperature, enough pressure turns the gas into liquid."],"others":"Above the critical temperature, liquefaction is impossible. 0 °C is not special. Being above the boiling point does not decide liquefaction by pressure.","answer":"below its critical temperature"},"chem-11-06-m19":{"idea":"Vapour pressure is the pressure of vapour in equilibrium with its liquid.","steps":["Heating gives molecules more kinetic energy.","More molecules escape from the liquid into the vapour.","So the vapour pressure increases, quickly, as the temperature rises."],"others":"It does not become zero or stay the same. It does not fall because molecules move slower; they move faster when heated.","answer":"increases, since more molecules have enough energy to escape"},"chem-11-06-m20":{"idea":"A liquid boils when its vapour pressure equals the external pressure.","steps":["On a mountain, the air pressure is lower.","The vapour pressure needs to reach this smaller value.","That happens at a lower temperature, so water boils below 100 °C."],"others":"The external pressure is lower on a mountain, not higher. Dissolved gas does not set the boiling point. Intermolecular forces in water do not change with height.","answer":"the external pressure is lower, so the vapour pressure equals it at a lower temperature"},"chem-11-06-m21":{"idea":"Heat gives molecules more energy to overcome the attractions between them.","steps":["With a rise in temperature, kinetic energy increases.","The intermolecular forces become less effective.","The liquid flows more easily, so viscosity decreases.","The surface is less tight, so surface tension decreases."],"others":"They do not both increase, or one rise while the other falls. They also do not stay the same. Both properties come from intermolecular forces, which weaken on heating.","answer":"both decrease"},"chem-11-06-m22":{"idea":"Dalton's law gives the total pressure of a gas mixture.","steps":["It applies to gases that do not react with each other.","Each gas acts as if it were alone in the vessel.","Total pressure = p₁ + p₂ + …, the sum of partial pressures.","Partial pressure = mole fraction × total pressure."],"others":"The total is not a product, an average, or the pressure of one gas. It is the sum. Also, the law needs gases that do not react.","answer":"do not react with each other, and the total pressure is the sum of the partial pressures"},"chem-11-06-n01":{"idea":"Boyle's law: for a fixed amount of gas at constant temperature, P₁V₁ = P₂V₂.","steps":["P₁ = 1.0 atm, V₁ = 2.0 L, V₂ = 0.50 L.","P₂ = P₁V₁ ÷ V₂ = 1.0 × 2.0 ÷ 0.50.","P₂ = 4.0 atm.","Check: volume is 4 times smaller, so pressure is 4 times larger."],"answer":"4.0 atm"},"chem-11-06-n02":{"idea":"Charles' law: at constant pressure, V₁ ÷ T₁ = V₂ ÷ T₂, with T in kelvin.","steps":["T₁ = 27 + 273.15 = 300.15 K. T₂ = 127 + 273.15 = 400.15 K.","V₂ = V₁ × T₂ ÷ T₁ = 3.0 × 400.15 ÷ 300.15.","V₂ = 4.000 L.","Check: the volume rose by a factor about 1.33, matching 400 ÷ 300."],"trap":"Never use °C in gas-law ratios.","answer":"4.000 L"},"chem-11-06-n03":{"idea":"Use the ideal gas equation PV = nRT and solve for n.","steps":["n = PV ÷ RT.","n = (2.0 × 5.0) ÷ (0.08206 × 300).","n = 10.0 ÷ 24.618 = 0.406 mol.","Check units: atm × L ÷ (L atm/(mol K) × K) gives mol."],"answer":"0.406 mol"},"chem-11-06-n04":{"idea":"The molar mass of a gas follows from its density using M = dRT/P.","steps":["d = 1.25 g/L, T = 273.15 K, P = 1.00 atm.","M = 1.25 × 0.08206 × 273.15 ÷ 1.00.","M = 28.0 g/mol, which matches N₂.","Check: 1 mol occupies 22.414 L here, and 1.25 × 22.414 = 28.0 g."],"answer":"28.0 g/mol"},"chem-11-06-n05":{"idea":"Use the combined gas law when pressure, volume and temperature all change.","steps":["P₁V₁ ÷ T₁ = P₂V₂ ÷ T₂.","P₂ = P₁V₁T₂ ÷ (T₁V₂) = 1.0 × 2.0 × 600 ÷ (300 × 1.0).","P₂ = 4.0 atm.","Check: halving the volume doubles P to 2.0 atm. Doubling T doubles it again to 4.0 atm."],"answer":"4.0 atm"},"chem-11-06-n06":{"idea":"The compressibility factor compares the real gas with an ideal gas: Z = PV ÷ nRT.","steps":["P = 10 atm, V = 2.0 L, n = 1, T = 300 K.","Z = (10 × 2.0) ÷ (1 × 0.08206 × 300).","Z = 20 ÷ 24.618 = 0.812.","Z is below 1, so attractions help compression."],"answer":"0.812"},"chem-11-06-n07":{"idea":"Graham's law: the rate of effusion is inversely proportional to √M.","steps":["Rate(H₂) ÷ Rate(O₂) = √(M of O₂ ÷ M of H₂).","= √(32 ÷ 2.0) = √16.","The ratio is 4.0.","So hydrogen effuses 4 times faster than oxygen."],"trap":"Lighter gas, faster rate.","answer":"4.0"},"chem-11-06-n08":{"idea":"Use PV = nRT with SI units to get pressure in pascal.","steps":["T = 25 + 273.15 = 298.15 K. V = 10 L = 0.010 m³.","P = nRT ÷ V = 0.50 × 8.314 × 298.15 ÷ 0.010.","P = 1.239 × 10⁵ Pa = 124 kPa.","Check: with R = 0.08206, P = 1.223 atm, and 1.223 × 101.3 = 124 kPa."],"trap":"Convert litres to m³ when R is 8.314.","answer":"124 kPa"}};
